-- Statement fix for Anki-NF-1176 (2026-11-14).
-- Statement now says 'usable hosts' because (2^n)-2 is the usable-hosts formula.
-- The explanation already said 'usable hosts'; only the statement was wrong.

UPDATE questions SET question = 'What is the formula to calculate the maximum number of usable hosts in a network? (Choose one answer)', options = '[\"A. (2^n)-1\", \"B. (2^n)-2 (n = number of host bits)\", \"C. 2^n\", \"D. 2^(n-2)\"]', correct_option = '[1]', explanation = '<div class=\"explanation-rich-content\">\n<div class=\"expl-card concept-card\"><div class=\"section-title-concept\">📌 Core Concept: Subnetting - Maximum Hosts Formula</div><p>The maximum number of usable hosts in a network is (2^n) - 2, where n is the number of host bits. You subtract 2 because the network address and the broadcast address cannot be assigned to hosts.</p></div>\n<div class=\"expl-card correct-card\"><h4 class=\"section-title-correct\">✓ Why the Correct Answer (Option B) is Correct:</h4><ul><li><span class=\"opt-tag correct\">Option B</span> <strong>(2^n)-2 (n = number of host bits)</strong>: (2^n)-2 (n = number of host bits) is correct because the formula takes 2 to the power of the host bits and subtracts the network and broadcast addresses.</li></ul></div>\n<div class=\"expl-card incorrect-card\"><h4 class=\"section-title-incorrect\">✗ Why Other Options are Incorrect:</h4><ul><li><span class=\"opt-tag wrong\">Option A</span> <strong>(2^n)-1</strong>: (2^n)-1 only removes one reserved address, but both the network address and the broadcast address are unusable.</li><li><span class=\"opt-tag wrong\">Option C</span> <strong>2^n</strong>: 2^n gives the total number of addresses, including the network and broadcast addresses. The usable host count subtracts those two.</li><li><span class=\"opt-tag wrong\">Option D</span> <strong>2^(n-2)</strong>: 2^(n-2) is not the host formula. It would give a far smaller number than the real usable host count.</li></ul></div>\n<div class=\"expl-card tip-card\"><h4 class=\"section-title-tip\">💡 CCNA Exam Tip:</h4><p>The &quot;-2&quot; is the classic trap: 2^n gives all addresses, but two of them (network and broadcast) are unusable, so it is (2^n) - 2.</p></div>\n<div class=\"expl-card concept-card\"><div class=\"section-title-concept\">📌 Source: Anki Flashcards - Network Fundamentals (CCNA / Day 08 Flashcards - IPv4 Addresses (Part 2)</div></div>\n</div>' WHERE question_no = 'Anki-NF-1176';
